   "source": [
    "# AI & Machine Learning: Code, Train & Deploy\n",
    "## Week 6 — Experimental Design and Recommendation Testing\n",
    "\n",
    "**Syllabus focus:** experimental design; sampling; power analysis; A/B testing.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Business context:** A platform cannot simply assume a fairer recommendation algorithm is better. It must test whether a new strategy improves discovery without destroying user engagement.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Capstone deliverable:** Experimental plan to compare a standard recommender with a fairness-aware recommender.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Learning objectives\n",
    "- define treatment and control groups\n",
    "- write proper research questions and hypotheses\n",
    "- choose a relevant success metric\n",
    "- explain the logic behind A/B testing\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Research question\n",
    "\n",
    "Does a fairness-aware recommendation model improve long-tail artist discovery without materially lowering user engagement?\n"
   ]
  },
  {
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "control = {'engagement_rate': 0.67, 'long_tail_share': 0.18, 'artist_diversity': 0.32}\n",
    "treatment = {'engagement_rate': 0.64, 'long_tail_share': 0.31, 'artist_diversity': 0.58}\n",
    "\n",
    "print('Control group:', control)\n",
    "print('Treatment group:', treatment)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Hypothesis examples\n",
    "\n",
    "- Null hypothesis: the fairness-aware recommender does not change long-tail exposure or engagement.\n",
    "- Alternative hypothesis: the fairness-aware recommender increases long-tail exposure with only a small decline in engagement.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Business metrics\n",
    "\n",
    "Recommended metrics for the experiment:\n",
    "- engagement rate\n",
    "- average listening time\n",
    "- share of recommendations from long-tail artists\n",
    "- genre diversity score\n",
    "- skip rate\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Example experiment design\n",
    "\n",
    "- Users are randomly assigned to control or treatment.\n",
    "- Both groups receive music recommendations for 30 days.\n",
    "- Success means a meaningful increase in long-tail exposure without large engagement loss.\n",
    "- Analysis can include a two-sample comparison or a conversion-based test.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "def summarize_test(control, treatment):\n",
    "    print('Engagement delta:', treatment['engagement_rate'] - control['engagement_rate'])\n",
    "    print('Long-tail delta:', treatment['long_tail_share'] - control['long_tail_share'])\n",
    "    print('Diversity delta:', treatment['artist_diversity'] - control['artist_diversity'])\n",
    "\n",
    "summarize_test(control, treatment)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Practice task\n",
    "Write a short experimental plan in bullets. Include at least one primary metric and one secondary metric.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Deliverable checkpoint\n",
    "This week should create a test plan suitable for a music product leader or experiment team.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Week 6 rubric\n",
    "- Hypotheses are clear and correctly framed: 25%\n",
    "- Experimental design is credible and realistic: 25%\n",
    "- Metrics are relevant to the business problem: 25%\n",
    "- Business interpretation is practical: 25%\n"
   ]
  }
